\fs96 \cf2 Python types\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 When calling Python methods, Mojo needs to convert back and forth between native Python objects and native Mojo objects. Most of these conversions happen automatically, but there are a number of cases that Mojo doesn't handle yet. In these cases you may need to do an explicit conversion, or call an extra method.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Mojo types in Python\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo primitive types implicitly convert into Python objects. Today we support integers, floats, booleans, and strings.\
To demonstrate, the following example dynamically creates an in-memory Python module named 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 py_utils
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  containing a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 type_printer()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function, which simply prints the type of a given value. Then you can see how different Mojo values convert into corresponding Python types.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 from\cf14  std\cf5 .\cf14 python \cf5 import\cf14  Python\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 main\cf5 ()\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     py_module \cf5 =\cf14  \cf16 """\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf16 \cb13 def type_printer(value) raises:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf16 \cb13     print(type(value))\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf16 \cb13 """\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf14 \cb13     py_utils \cf5 =\cf14  Python\cf5 .\cf14 evaluate\cf5 (\cf14 py_module\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf15 file\cf5 =\cf17 True\cf5 ,\cf14  name\cf5 =\cf16 "py_utils"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     py_utils\cf5 .\cf14 type_printer\cf5 (\cf17 4\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     py_utils\cf5 .\cf14 type_printer\cf5 (\cf17 3.14\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     py_utils\cf5 .\cf14 type_printer\cf5 (\cf17 True\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     py_utils\cf5 .\cf14 type_printer\cf5 (\cf16 "Mojo"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 <class 'int'>\cb1 \
\cb13 <class 'float'>\cb1 \
\cb13 <class 'bool'>\cb1 \
\cb13 <class 'str'>\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Python types in Mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can also create and use Python objects from Mojo.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Mojo wrapper objects\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 When you use Python objects in your Mojo code, Mojo adds the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/python/python_object/PythonObject/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 PythonObject}} wrapper around the Python object. This object exposes a number of common double underscore methods (dunder methods) like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __getitem__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __getattr__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , passing them through to the underlying Python object. Most of the time, you can treat the wrapped object just like you'd treat it in Python. You can use dot-notation to access attributes and call methods, and use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 []
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  operator to access an item in a sequence.\
You can explicitly create a wrapped Python object by initializing a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 PythonObject
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  with a Mojo integer, float, boolean, or string. Additionally, you can create several types of Python collections directly in Mojo using the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/python/python/Python/#dict"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 Python.dict()}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/python/python/Python/#list"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 Python.list()}}, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/python/python/Python/#tuple"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 Python.tuple()}} static methods.\
For example, to create a Python dictionary, use the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/python/python/Python/#dict"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 Python.dict()}} method:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 from\cf14  std\cf5 .\cf14 python \cf5 import\cf14  Python\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 main\cf5 ()\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     py_dict \cf5 =\cf14  Python\cf5 .\cf15 dict\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     py_dict\cf5 [\cf16 "item_name"\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  \cf16 "whizbang"\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     py_dict\cf5 [\cf16 "price"\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  \cf17 11.75\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     py_dict\cf5 [\cf16 "inventory"\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  \cf17 100\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 py_dict\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 \{'item_name': 'whizbang', 'price': 11.75, 'inventory': 100\}\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 With the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/python/python/Python/#list"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 Python.list()}} method, you can create a Python list and optionally initialize it:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 from\cf14  std\cf5 .\cf14 python \cf5 import\cf14  Python\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 main\cf5 ()\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     py_list \cf5 =\cf14  Python\cf5 .\cf15 list\cf5 (\cf16 "cat"\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf17 2\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf17 3.14159\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf17 4\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     n \cf5 =\cf14  py_list\cf5 [\cf17 2\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf16 "n ="\cf5 ,\cf14  n\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     py_list\cf5 .\cf14 append\cf5 (\cf17 5\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     py_list\cf5 [\cf17 0\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  \cf16 "aardvark"\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 py_list\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 n = 3.14159\cb1 \
\cb13 ['aardvark', 2, 3.14159, 4, 5]\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/python/python/Python/#tuple"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 Python.tuple()}} method creates a Python tuple of values:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 from\cf14  std\cf5 .\cf14 python \cf5 import\cf14  Python\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 main\cf5 ()\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     py_tuple \cf5 =\cf14  Python\cf5 .\cf15 tuple\cf5 (\cf16 "cat"\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf17 2\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf17 3.1415\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 "cat"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     n \cf5 =\cf14  py_tuple\cf5 [\cf17 2\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf16 "n ="\cf5 ,\cf14  n\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf16 "Number of cats:"\cf5 ,\cf14  py_tuple\cf5 .\cf14 count\cf5 (\cf16 "cat"\cf5 ))\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 n = 3.1415\cb1 \
\cb13 Number of cats: 2\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 If you want to construct a Python type that doesn't have a literal Mojo equivalent, you can also use the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/python/python/Python/#evaluate"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 Python.evaluate()}} method. For example, to create a Python 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 set
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 from\cf14  std\cf5 .\cf14 python \cf5 import\cf14  Python\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 main\cf5 ()\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  py_set \cf5 =\cf14  Python\cf5 .\cf14 evaluate\cf5 (\cf16 '\{2, 3, 2, 7, 11, 3\}'\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     num_items \cf5 =\cf14  \cf15 len\cf5 (\cf14 py_set\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 num_items\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 "items in the set."\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     contained \cf5 =\cf14  \cf17 7\cf14  \cf5 in\cf14  py_set\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf16 "Is 7 in the set:"\cf5 ,\cf14  contained\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 4 items in the set.\cb1 \
\cb13 Is 7 in the set: True\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf10 \cb12 PythonObject
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  implements the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/format/Writable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 Writable}} trait. This allows you to print Python values using the built-in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/io/print/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 print()}} function, as shown in several of the previous examples.\
However, most other Mojo APIs don't accept 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 PythonObject
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values directly. In these cases you'll need to explicitly convert a Python value into a native Mojo value. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 from\cf14  std\cf5 .\cf14 python \cf5 import\cf14  Python\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 from\cf14  std\cf5 .\cf14 python \cf5 import\cf14  PythonObject\cb1 \
\
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 main\cf5 ()\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  py_string \cf5 =\cf14  PythonObject\cf5 (\cf16 "Hello, Mojo!"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  py_bool \cf5 =\cf14  PythonObject\cf5 (\cf17 True\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  py_int \cf5 =\cf14  PythonObject\cf5 (\cf17 123\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  py_float \cf5 =\cf14  PythonObject\cf5 (\cf17 3.14\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  mojo_string \cf5 =\cf14  String\cf5 (\cf14 py\cf5 =\cf14 py_string\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  mojo_bool \cf5 =\cf14  Bool\cf5 (\cf14 py\cf5 =\cf14 py_bool\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  mojo_int \cf5 =\cf14  Int\cf5 (\cf14 py\cf5 =\cf14 py_int\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  mojo_float \cf5 =\cf14  Float64\cf5 (\cf14 py\cf5 =\cf14 py_float\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Comparing Python types in Mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can use Python objects in Mojo comparison expressions, and the Mojo 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 is
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  operator also works to compare the identity of two Python objects. Python values like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 False
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 None
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  evaluate as false in Mojo boolean expressions as well.\
If you need to know the type of the underlying Python object, you can use the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/python/python/Python/#type"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 Python.type()}} method, which is equivalent to the Python 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 type()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  builtin. You can test if a Python object is of a particular type by performing an identity comparison against the type as shown below:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 from\cf14  std\cf5 .\cf14 python \cf5 import\cf14  Python\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 main\cf5 ()\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  value1 \cf5 =\cf14  PythonObject\cf5 (\cf17 3.7\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     value2 \cf5 =\cf14  Python\cf5 .\cf14 evaluate\cf5 (\cf16 "10/3"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf6 # Compare values\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf16 "Is value1 greater than 3:"\cf5 ,\cf14  value1 \cf5 >\cf14  \cf17 3\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf16 "Is value1 greater than value2:"\cf5 ,\cf14  value1 \cf5 >\cf14  value2\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf6 # Compare identities\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     value3 \cf5 =\cf14  value2\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf16 "value1 is value2:"\cf5 ,\cf14  value1 \cf5 is\cf14  value2\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf16 "value2 is value3:"\cf5 ,\cf14  value2 \cf5 is\cf14  value3\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf6 # Compare types\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     py_float_type \cf5 =\cf14  Python\cf5 .\cf14 evaluate\cf5 (\cf16 "float"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf16 "Python float type:"\cf5 ,\cf14  py_float_type\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf16 "value1 type:"\cf5 ,\cf14  Python\cf5 .\cf15 type\cf5 (\cf14 value1\cf5 ))\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf16 "Is value1 a Python float:"\cf5 ,\cf14  Python\cf5 .\cf15 type\cf5 (\cf14 value1\cf5 )\cf14  \cf5 is\cf14  py_float_type\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
